    "ls|LS16_S9_U_100": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 100 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18-07-2016 \n \nEstablishment of IES  \n \n100. SHRI DUSHYANT CHAUTALA: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the Government has constituted any Committee to explore the possibilities \nfor establishment of Indian Education Service (IES); \n(b) If so, whether the said Committee has also submitted its recommendations in this \nregard; \n(c) If so, the details thereof and the salient features of recommendations; and \n(d) The follow-up actions proposed by the Government on the recommendations of the \nCommittee? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (d) : National Education Policies of 1968 and 1986 (modified in 1992) provide for \nformation of Indian Education Service as an  All India Service. However, there was no \nconsensus among the State Governments and as such the proposal was dropped . The \nCommittee constituted for Evolution of th e New Education Policy has comprehensively \nexamined the matter and recommended for the establishment of Indian Education \nService.  Recommendation of the Committee has been incorporated in Some Inputs for \nDraft National Education Policy, 2016 which is available at MHRD’s website.  \n \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_102": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†102 \nANSWERED ON 18.07.2016 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF CROSS BORDER TOURISM \n \n†102. DR. MANOJ RAJORIA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any pr oposal to develop cross \nborder tourism with the neighbouring countries through the \ndevelopment of International Tourism Circuit and if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(b)  whether the Government is considering to develop tourist \nspots through Public-Private-Partnership (PPP) Model and if so, \nthe amount allocated by the Government for this purpose \nduring the last three years; and  \n(c)  whether the inflow of tourists in the North East Region (NER) \nis very low in comparison to other parts of the country and if \nso, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a): The Ministry of Tourism, Government of India collaborates \nwith its neighbouring countries for promotion of tourism \ndestinations and circuits. As an o utcome of the 5th SAARC Tourism \nworking Group meeting held on 25th - 26th November 2015 at New \nDelhi, Ministry of Tourism has formulated itineraries based on \nthemes such as Buddhist Circuit, Archaeological Sites, Adventure \nand Eco Tourism and has shared th e same with the SAARC \nSecretariat through MEA. During the 1st Joint Working Group \nmeeting on tourism cooperation between India and Nepal both \ncountries agreed to cooperate for promotion of Buddhist Circuit, \nHindu Circuit and Adventure Tourism. During the J oint Working \n\nGroup meetings between India and Sri Lanka it has been agreed to \nassist each other in carrying out joint collaboration on the Buddhist \nCircuit and promote Ramayana trail.  \n \n(b): Ministry of Tourism provides Central Financial Assistance \n(CFA) under Public -Private-Partnership Model in the Large Revenue \nGenerating Scheme to the cap of Rs.25 crore for the development of \ntourist trains, cruise vessels, golf course etc. During the last three \nyears one project “Integrated Cable Car and Lakeside Touris m \nDevelopment project at Loktak Lake Manipur” was sanctioned under \nPPP mode/LRG Scheme for an amount of Rs. 20.35 crore. \n \n(c): There has been an overall growth in the number of total tourist \narrivals/visits (foreign and domestic) in the North East Region i n the \nyear 2015 as compared to 2014. The total tourist arrivals/visits in \nthe North East Region were 8059306 in 2015 as compared to \n7162562 in the year 2014, as per details at Annexure. \n \n********* \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART  (c) OF LOK SABHA UNST ARRED \nQUESTION NO. †102 ANSWERED ON 18.07.2016 REGARDING  \nDEVELOPMENT OF CROSS BORDER TOURISM. \n \nDetails of Tourists Arrivals in North East Region (NER) in the year \n2014 and 2015 \n \n \nSl. \nNo. \n \nStates \nTOURIST ARRIVAL IN 2014 \n \nTOURIST ARRIVAL IN \n2015 \nDomestic Foreign Total Domestic Foreign Total \n1 Arunachal \nPradesh \n335974 5204 341178 352067 5705 357772 \n2 Assam 4826702 21537 4848239 5491845 24720 5516565 \n3 Manipur 115499 2769 118268 146169 3260 149429 \n4 Meghalaya 716469 8664 725133 751165 8027 759192 \n5 Mizoram 68203 921 69124 66605 798 67403 \n6 Nagaland 58507 2585 61092 64616 2769 67385 \n7 Sikkim 562418 49175 611593 705023 38479 743502 \n8 Tripura 361247 26688 387935 363172 34886 398058 \n      7162562   8059306 \n \n********* \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_106": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n(MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.106 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.07.2016 \n \nNATIONAL COMMISSION FOR ST \n \n*106. ADV. CHINTAMAN NAVASHA WANAGA:  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the extent to which the National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST) has been \nsuccessful in overseeing the implementation of various safeguards provided to \nscheduled tribes under the Constitution of India;  \n(b) the eligibility criteria laid down by the Gov ernment for appointment of members of \nNCST;  \n(c) whether the members of the Commission are appointed from the ST community; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nUNION MINISTER OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS(SHRI JUAL ORAM)  \n \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED Q No. 106 \nBY ADV. CHINTAMAN NAVASHA WANAGA REGARDING “NATIONAL COMMISSION \nFOR ST” FOR ANSWER ON 25.07.2016 \n \n(a):Article 338 A Clause (5) (a) of Indian Constitution bestows power onNational \nCommission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST) to investigate matters relating to safeguards \nprovided for Scheduled Tribes (STs) under the Constitution of India or under different \nstatutes. The recommendations made by the Commission in such matters are submitted \nin its Annual Reports to the Hon’ble President of India. \nPresident causes such reports to be laid in each House of Parliament along with a \nmemorandum explaining action taken or proposed to be taken and reasons for non -\nacceptance of these recommendations, if any, concerning matter of Union. \nRecommendations concerning State Government are forwarded to concerned State \nGovernor who shall cause it to be laid before legislature of State along with action \ntaken, proposed to be taken and reasons for non-acceptance, if any. \nTill 15.07.2016, NCST, since its constitution in 2004, has submitted Annual Reports (as \nAnnexed) to the President of India.  \n(b)to (d):NCST consist of Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson and three other Members who \nare appointed by the President by warrant under his hand and seal.  Chairperson shall \nbe from amongst eminent social -political workers belonging to S Ts, who inspire \nconfidence amongst the S Ts by their very personality and record of selfless service. \nMembers of the Commission shall be appointed from amongst persons of ability, \nintegrity and standing who have had a record of selfless service to the cause of justice \nfor STs. Out of the Vice-Chairperson and three other membersat least two (of four) shall \nbe from amongst persons belonging to S Ts and a t least one (of four) member shall be \nfrom amongst women. \n\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No.*106 b y \nAdv. ChintamanNavashaWanaga for answer on 25.7.2016 \n \nStatus of Annual Reports of National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST) \n \nS. No. Report Period (year) Date of Submission to \nPresident \n \n1. 1st 2004-05 and 2005-06 08.08.2006 \n \n2. 2nd 2006-07 03.09.2008 \n \n3. 3rd 2007-08 29.03.2010 \n \n4. 4th 2008-09 27.08.2010 \n \n5. 5th 2009-10 13.07.2011 \n \n6. Special May 2012 18.06.2012 \n \n7. 6th 2010-11 25.10.2013 \n \n8. 7th 2011-12 20.02.2015 \n \n9. 8th 2012-13 16.11.2015 \n \n10. 9th 2013-14 24.05.2016 \n \n11. 10th 2014-15 31.05.2016 \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_108": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 108 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.07.2016 \n \nOnline Content \n*108.  SHRI HARI OM PANDAY: \n           SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has signed any  Memorandum of Understanding with non-\nGovernmental bodies for providing \"Digital/Online Content\" throughout the day and free of \ncost for the benefit of students and if so, the details and the salient features thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Ministry proposes to launch Massive Open Online Courses (MOOC) \nplatform 'Swayam' to provide high quality education on various subjects covering all \ndisciplines and also dedicated 'Prashikshak', a teacher education portal in the country and \nif so, the details and the salient features thereof; \n \n(c) the time by which the said facilities are likely to be launched; and \n \n(d) the manner and extent to which MOOC would be beneficial for th e education system \nas well as to the students? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \nPart (a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n*** \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO  PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 108 ASKED BY  SHRI HARI OM  PANDAY AND  \nSHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV REGARDING ONLINE CONTENT \n \n(a) No Madam, Ministry has not signed Me morandum of Understanding (MoU) with any \nnon-governmental body for providing “Digital/ Online Content”. \n \n(b) and (c)  In order to provide the best t eaching-learning resour ces to the students \nacross the country, Government is launc hing Massive Open Online Courses (MOOCs) \nthrough Online Learning Portal named SWAYAM. Through this, it would be possible for \nany student or any school drop-out  to join virtual classes o ffered by the best teachers in \nthe Country, interact with the teacher, take tests, earn academic credits and transfer them \non their academic record. The courses o ffered in this portal will cover 9\nth Class to Post \nGraduation levels. SWAYAM woul d provide one stop web location for interactive e-\ncontent for all courses having facility of peer group interact ion and discussion forums to \nclarify doubts. SWAYAM and a number of MOOCs are likely to be launched soon. \n \nGovernment has launched 'Prashikshak', a teacher education portal on 30th June 2016 for \nbettering the standards of District Institutes  of Education and Tr aining (DIETs) and \nteachers training.   \n \n(d)  The Massive Open Online Courses (M OOCs) offered through SWAYAM will be \ninteractive and prepared by the best teachers in the country. Courses delivered through \nSWAYAM will be available free of cost to the learners,  however students wanting \ncertifications shall be register ed and offered a certificate on successful completion of the \ncourse, with a little fee. At the end of eac h course, there will be an assessment of the \nstudent through proctored examination and the credits secured in this exam could be \ntransferred to the academic record of the students. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_109": "1\n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.109\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.7.2016\n \nSRAVANA 3, 1938 (SAKA)\n \n \n \nANCIENT WORSHIP PLACES\n \n \n \n+*109.\n \nSHRI  KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:\n \nSHRI DEVENDRA SINGH BHOLE:\n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be \npleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government has identified ancient places of worships in the country \nand if so, the location\n-\nwise details of such places in the country;\n \n(b)\n \nwhether the ancient places of worships located in different parts of the  \ncountry \nhave not been covered under the protected list of the Archaeological Survey of \nIndia, if so, the reasons therefor;\n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to renovate such ancient sites and temples \nand declared them as places of tourist interest and i\nf so, the details thereof, \nState/UT\n-\nwise; and\n \n(d)\n \nthe details of funds sanctioned and utilized for the renovation and restoration of \nidentified ancient temples and sites in the country, State/UT\n-\nwise? \n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE AND TOURISM (I\nNDEPENDENT CHARGE)\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n(a)to(d)\n \nA Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n2\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.109 FOR 25.7.2016\n \n \n(a)\n \nArchaeological Survey of India identifies new places/sites including \nmonuments (religious, non\n-\nreligious and others) through various means \nsuch as village to village survey through its field offices. \n \n \nIn this process in \nthe year 2015\n-\n16 in all \n1820\n \nvillag\nes were surveyed out of which \n822\n \nhave \nrevealed remains of archaeological significance including 386 places of \nworship. \n \nA State\n-\nwise abstract indicating p\nlaces of worship is at \nAnnexure\n–\n \nI. \n \n \n(b)\n \nOur country has \nin\n-\nnumerable number of places of worship a\nll over the \ncountry, out of which \na \nfew of them have been declared protected.  \nArchaeological Survey of India protects only such monuments which meet \nthe criteria specified under Section 2 of Ancient Monuments and \nArchaeological Sites and Remains Act, 1958\n.\n \n \n(c)\n \nIn addition to conservation of protected monuments, Archaeological \nSurvey of India takes up conservation of religious places, not protected by \nArchaeological Survey of India, as a Special work. Most of the religious \nplaces are visited by large numb\ner of devotees, scholars besides \nenthusiast and as such they are places of tourist interest as well.  \nHowever, there is no provision to declare such places as of tourist interest \nunder the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act, \n1958. \n \n \n(d)\n \nThe expenditure incurred on conservation, preservation and restoration of \ncentrally protected monuments (including temples and sites) during the last \nthree years and provisions made for this year is at Annexure \n–\nII.  Further, \nexpenditure incurred on \nconservation, preservation and restoration of non\n-\nprotected monuments/religious places is at Annexure\n-\nIII. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n3\n \n \n \n \nANNEXURE \n–\nI\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.109\n \nFOR 25.7.2016\n \n \nSTATEMENT SHOWING PLACES SURVEYED BY THE ARCHAEOLOGICAL \nSURVEY OF INDIA AND NUMBER OF RELIGIOUS PLACES IDENTIFIED DURING \nTHE YEAR 2015\n-\n16 \n \n \nSl.\n \nNo.\n \nState\n \nTotal \nvillages \nsurveyed\n \nNumber of villages \nyielding antiquarian \nremains\n \nNumber of \nreligious places \nidentified \n \n1.\n \nBihar\n \n60\n \n3\n \n00\n \n2.\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n67\n \n10\n \n03\n \n3.\n \nGoa\n \n50\n \n50\n \n12\n \n4.\n \nGujarat\n \n83\n \n54\n \n \n33\n \n5.\n \nHimachal \nPradesh\n \n237\n \n85\n \n61\n \n6.\n \nJharkhand\n \n98\n \n71\n \n25\n \n7.\n \nKarnataka\n \n237\n \n226\n \n169\n \n8.\n \nMaharashtra\n \n116\n \n58\n \n23\n \n9.\n \nMadhya \nPradesh\n \n142\n \n106\n \n13\n \n10.\n \nOdisha\n \n29\n \n12\n \n14\n \n11.\n \nPunjab\n \n207\n \n21\n \n01\n \n12.\n \nRajasthan\n \n04\n \n04\n \n00\n \n13.\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n218\n \n82\n \n27\n \n14.\n \nUttar \nPradesh\n \n155\n \n72\n \n00\n \n15.\n \nUttarakhand\n \n189\n \n10\n \n05\n \n \nTOTAL\n \n1820\n \n822\n \n386\n \n \n\n4\n \n \n \n                                                                                                \n                                                                                     \nANNEXURE\n-\nII\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.109 FOR \n25.07.2016 \n \nSTATE/UT\n-\nWISE DETAILS OF FUNDS ALLOCATED AND UTILISED  FOR CONSERVATION OF MONUMENTS UNDER ASI \nFOR THE LAST FIVE YEARS                                           \n                                                                                                                             \n                                                                                                                             \n(Amount Rs. in Lakhs)\n \nSl. \nNo.\n \nName of \nState/UT\n \nNumber of \nprotected \nmonuments\n \nCircle / Branch\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n1.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n743\n \nAgra Circle\n \n957.97\n \n1404.99\n \n1270.00\n \n1060.00\n \nLucknow Circle\n \n944.99\n \n1165.00\n \n945.08\n \n925.00\n \nSarnath Circle\n \n---\n \n534.99\n \n495.00\n \n500.00\n \n2.\n \nMaharashtra\n \n285\n \nAurangabad Circle\n \n493.00\n \n695.00\n \n629.05\n \n500.00\n \nMumbai Circle\n \n415.00\n \n645.00\n \n902.82\n \n725.00\n \nNagpur Circle\n \n---\n \n444.98\n \n845.00\n \n610.00\n \n3.\n \nKarnataka\n \n506\n \nBangalore Circle\n \n1253.00\n \n1515.99\n \n1427.02\n \n1125.00\n \nMini Circle Hampi\n \n--\n \n90.00\n \n401.24\n \n5