    "ls|LS16_S9_U_174": "   \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.174 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2016 \n \n \n“ILLEGAL MINING”  \n \n \n174. SHRI RABINDRA KUMAR JENA AND SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \n \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the illegal mining activities across the \ncountry, if so, the details thereof indicatin g number of reported cases of illegal \nmining during each of the last three years, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b) the estimated revenue loss incurred to  the Government therefrom and the \nnumber of officials reportedly killed/died while checking the illegal mining \nduring the said period, State/UT-wise; \n \n(c) the action taken by the Gove rnment against the persons involved in the illegal \nmining and the extent of success achieved in this regard; \n \n(d) the other corrective steps being taken/proposed to be taken by the Government \nto check illegal mining and for the safe ty of the Government employees in the \ncountry; and \n \n(e) whether the Government is considering to adopt satellite technique to put a \ncheck on illegal sand-mining, if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, COAL, \nNEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) State Governments are empowered, un der Section 23C of the Mines and \nMineral (Development and Re gulation) Act, 1957 ( MMDR Act, 1957) to make rules \nfor prevention of illegal mining, transportation and storage of minerals; and, therefore, \nmatters relating to illega l mining come under the legislative and administrative \njurisdiction of State Governments. \n \n As per quarterly returns submitted by the State Governments on illegal mining \nto India Bureau of Mines(IBM) (a subordinate office under the Ministry of Mines), the \ncases of illegal mining for major and minor minerals reported in the country for the \nyears 2013-14 to 2015-16 (Quarter ending December 2015) are at Annexure-I to \nAnnexure-III. \n \n(b) As per quarterly returns submitted by the State Governments on illegal mining \nto IBM, the value of mineral reported agains t the number of cases detected for illegal \nmining are at Annexure-I to Annexure-III. \n \n \n\n                          \n    \n(c) As per Section 23C of Mines and Mine rals (Development and Regulation) Act \n1957, State governments are empowered to fra me rules for curbing illegal mining and \nto take action in the matter. However, as stated in reply to (a) & (b) above, based on \nthe quarterly returns received from the St ate Governments., total numbers of FIR \nlodged, Court Cases filed and Fine realized for the years 2013-14 to 2015-16 (Quarter \nending December 2015) is provided below: \n \n \nYear \n \nMajor minerals Minor minerals Total \n(Major + Minor) minerals \n FIR \nlodged \n(Nos.) \nCourt \ncases \nfiled \n(No) \nFine \nrealized \n(Rs. Lacs) \nFIR \nlodged \n(Nos.) \nCourt \ncases \nfiled \n(Nos.) \nFine \nrealized (Rs. \nLacs) \nFIR \nlodged \n(Nos.) \nCourt \ncases \nfiled \n(Nos.) \nFine \nrealized (Rs. \nLacs) \n2013-14 518 258 798.26 3331 10666 23107.93 3849 10924 23906.19 \n2014-15 404 227 1563.59 3835 9057 29032.24 4239 9284 30595.83 \n2015-16 \n(Quarter \nending \nDecember \n2015) \n711 282 572.41 4079 13945 27599.53 4790 14227 28171.94 \n \n \n \n(d)  The MMDR Act, 1957 was amended through the MMDR Amendment Act, \n2015 which came into effect from 12 th January, 2015. The Amen dment Act has, inter \nalia, provided for stringent punitive provis ions for combating illegal mining. Illegal \nmining has been made punishable with im prisonment for a term which may extend to \nfive years and with fine which may extend to five lakh rupees per hectare of the area. \nProvisions have been made for setting up of Special Courts for the purpose of \nproviding speedy trial of offences relating to illegal mining.  \n \n \n \n(e) Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM), Nagpur has entered into a MoU with National \nRemote Sensing Centre (NRSC), Hyderabad on 21.01.2016 for a pilot project “Sudoor \nDrushti” to demonstrate the feasibility of using High Resolution Satellite imagery and \nDigital Elevation Model (DEM ) in monitoring mining activities / changes over a \nperiod of time over selected group of mines.   \n \nThe Ministry of Mines has also engaged with Bhaskaracharya Institute of \nSpace Applications & Geo-Informatics (BISAG), Gujarat to develop a ‘Mine \nSurveillance System’ (MSS) for major minerals in consultation with Indian Bureau of \nMines to enhance the mechanis m of detecting illegal mining  with the use of satellite \nbased technology.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n                          \n    \nANNEXURE –I\n  RETURN ON ILLEGAL MINING FOR THE \nYEAR 2013-14 \n Major Minerals \nSR. STATE No. of  \nQuantum \nof \nmineral/ Value of  FIR Court \nFine \nrealised \n(Rs. Lakh) \nNo.  cases \nOre \nexcavate\nd /  Mineral / \nLodg\ned Cases \n    \nstacked / \nTransport\ned Ore  \n(Nos.\n) Filed \n    \n(in lakh \ntonnes) (Rs.Lacks)   (Nos.) \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 486 0.064 36.380 2 0 165.70\n2 \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH 0 0.000 0.000 0 0 0.00\n3 ASSAM 0 0.000 0.00 0 0 0.00\n4 CHHATTISGARH 208 0.020 45.84 2 135 80.61\n5 GOA 1 0.000 0.000 0 0 0.00\n6 GUJARAT 270 0.934 257.560 8 0 189.55\n7 HARYANA 0 0.000 0.000 0 0 0.00\n8 JHARKHAND 161 0.137 228.240 129 1 1.04\n9 KARNATAKA 342 0.127 16.260 5 5 82.31\n10 KERALA 18 0.008 4.830 0 0 5.36\n11 MADHYA PRADESH* 112 0.000 180.395 0 112 27.43\n12 MAHARASHTRA 0 0.000 0.000 0 0 0.00\n13 MIZORAM 0 0.000 0.000 0 0 0.00\n14 ODISHA 75 2.778 31.75 0 4 54.4\n15 RAJASTHAN 607 0.778 42.852 372 1 154.11\n16 SIKKIM 0 0.000 0.000 0 0 0.00\n17 TAMILNADU 6 1.378 207.535 0 0 37.75\n18 UTTARPRADESH 0 0.000 0.000 0 0 0.00\nTotal   2286 6.2244 1051.642 518 258 798.26\nNote  Quantity of mineral \n/ore \nexcavated/stacked/tran\nsported given in \nCu.mt/other unit \nMadhya \nPrades\nh* \n56705.81 \ncu.mt  \n