    "ls|LS16_S9_U_91": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO.91\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2016.\n \nASHADHA 27, 1938 (SAKA)\n \n \nBRINGING BACK OF ANTIQUITIES\n \n \n \n+ \n91. SHRI ALOK SANJAR:\n \n \n \nWill the \nMinister of CULTURE \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether  t\nhe Go\nvernment \nhas made any list of antique \nitems/statues/idols of national importance which are kept abroad in \nmuseums of  different countries; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details  thereof and the action taken by the Government in \nthis regard; \n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether  the G\novernment has received some antique items from \nvarious foreign countries; and \n \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE\n, \nCULTURE AND\n \nTOURISM (INDEPENDENT \nCHARGE)\n \n \n(DR\n. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n \n(a)\n \n&\n \n(b)\n \nArchaeological Survey of India maint\nains only list of those \nantiquities which have been sent abroad under Temporary Export \nPermit by the Director General, Archaeological Survey of India. \nInformation in respect of the antiquities abroad, not sent through \nArchaeological Survey of India, is not\n \navailable.\n \n(c)\n \n& \n \n(d)\n \nThe Government of India has retrieved twenty eight antiquities from \nforeign country (Annexure I).\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure I\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY PART TO (c) & (d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.91 FOR 18.07.20\n16\n \n \nLIST OF RETRIEVAL OF ANTIQUITIES FROM FOREIGN COUNTRIES.\n \n \n \nSl.No.\n \nDescription of object\n \nProvenance\n \nCountry from \nwhich \nretrieved\n \nDate/\n \nyear of \nRetrieval\n \n1.\n \nSawn stucco Head\n \nNalanda, Bihar\n \nU.K. and \nFrance\n \n1976\n \n2.\n \nNataraja of Chola period\n \nSivapuram Tami\nl Nadu\n \nU.S.A.\n \n1986\n \n3.\n \nTerracotta Yakashi of \nTamluk\n \nWest Bengal\n \nU.K.\n \n1986\n \n4.\n \nNataraja of Chola period\n \nTiruvilakkadi, Tamil Nadu\n \nU.S.A\n \n1986\n \n5.\n \nNataraja \nof Chola period\n \nPathur, Tamil Nadu\n \nU.K.\n \n1991\n \n6.\n \nTerracotta figures from \nBhitargaon\n \nUtter Pradesh\n \nU.S.A\n \n1991\n \n7.\n \nAmin pillars\n \nAmin, Haryana\n \nU.K\n \n1979\n-\n80\n \n8.\n \nImage of Buddha\n \nBodhgaya, Bihar\n \nU.S.A\n \n1999\n \n9.\n \nSculpture of \nKrishnajanma\n \nDhubela Museum \nChattarpur (M.P.)\n \nU.S.A\n \n1999\n \n10.\n \nPaintings\n \ni)\nChandigarh      Museum\n \nii) Chandigarh Museum\n \nU.S.A\n \nU.S.A\n \n10\n-\n1\n-\n1990\n \n4\n-\n3\n-\n1979\n \n11.\n \nImage of Lakulisa\n \nJageswar\n \nU.S.A\n \n2000\n \n12.\n \nDecorative Wooden \npanels (7 nos.)\n \nRajasthan\n \nHoland\n \n2001\n \n13.\n \nRetrieval of  Yogini \nVrishanana \n \n--\n \nParis\n \n2013\n \n14.\n \nNataraja \n \nTamil Nadu\n \nAustralia\n \n2014\n \n15\n \nArdhanareeswar form \nAustralia\n \nTamil Nadu \n \nAustra\nlia\n \n2014\n \n\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n16.\n \nParrot Lady\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \nCanada\n \n2015\n \n17.\n \nDurga (Mahishmardini)\n \nJ&K\n \nGermany\n \n2015\n \n18.\n \nUma Parameshwari\n \nTamil Nadu\n \nSingapore\n \n2015\n \n19.\n \nBronze image of Saint \nManikkavachaka\n \nTamil Nadu \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n20.\n \nMetal image of \nGanesha\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n21.\n \nFemale Figure \n(Honolulu Museum) \n \nEastern India \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n22.\n \nMale Deity (Honolulu \nMuseum)\n \nMadhya Pradesh \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n23.\n \nFloral Tile, Harvan \n \nKashmir \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n24.\n \nSri Devi, Chola Period\n \n \nTamil Nadu \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n25.\n \nBahubali Probab\nly \n \nAndhra Pradesh \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n26.\n \nMetal image of Parvati \n \nTamil Nadu \n \nU.S.A\n \n2016\n \n27.\n \nBronze image of \nBhoodevi\n \nTamil Nadu\n \nU.S.A.\n \n2016\n \n28.\n \nBronze image of \nChakkrathalwar \n \n \nTamil Nadu\n \nU.S.A.\n \n2016\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_94": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 94\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016\nINTERNATIONAL YOGA DAY\n†*94. SHRI RAVINDRA KUMAR PANDEY:\nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of programmes organized and projects launched on the occasion of the\nInternational Yoga Day;\n(b) the expenditure incurred for the celebration of the event this year including\nexpenses incurred on promotion, publicity, advertisement and awareness in India\nand worldwide;\n(c) the quantum of the money spent by the Government and other private players in\nthis regard;\n(d) the State -wise details of the number of participants in these programmes and\nplaces where these were held; and\n(e) the details of the proposals sent by various States/UTs regarding the selection of\nvenues and other facilities for organising the next International Yoga Day?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO 94 FOR 22ND JULY, 2016\n(a)The second International Day of Yoga was celebrated by organising various\nevents concerning yoga including mass yoga demonstration at National and\nInternational level.  The main event of mass yoga demonstration was organized at\nCapitol Complex, Chandigarh  on 21 st June, 2016.  Hon’ble Prime Minister\ndeclared two Awards - International and National Awards for outstanding\ncontribution in Yoga.   Hon’ble Prime Minister also declared to focus on yoga for\ndiabetes during the current year.\nA two-day International Conference on Yoga for Body and Beyond was\norganized on 22nd & 23rd June 2016 at Vigyan Bhawan, New Delhi.\nA National Yoga Olympiad  was jointly organized by National Council of\nEducational  Research  and Training (NCERT) under the Ministry of Human\nResource Development  and Ministry of Ayurveda, Yoga & Naturopathy, Unani,\nSiddha  and Homoeopathy (AYUSH).\n191 United Nations (UN) Member -States celebrated the 2 nd International\nDay of Yoga spread across an entire week from June 18-26, 2016.\n(b)&(c) The expenditure earmarked for various activities on promotion,\npublicity, advertisement and awareness of Yoga during 2 nd International Day\nof Yoga events  by Ministry of AYUSH and Ministry of External Affairs are\nas under: -\n(I) Ministry of AYUSH Rs. 21,59,74,485/-\n(II) Ministry of External Affairs Rs.   8,00,00,000/-\nThe Ministry of AYUSH did not provide funds to any State/UT for\ncelebration of International Day of Yoga 2016 except UT Administration of\nChandigarh for main event.  The money spent by the other private players are not\nmaintained.\n(d) The participation in the yoga events for celebration of International Day of\nYoga was on voluntary basis by all State/UTs at the State, District, Block and\nPanchayat level, Govt. and Educational Institutions, Corporates, Yoga Schools,\nNGOs, RWAs  etc.  No detail of the places and the number of participants has\nbeen maintained.\n(e) No proposals have been received from States/UTs for organizing of the next\nInternational Day of Yoga.",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_95": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 95\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016\nPOTASSIUM BROMATE IN BREADS\n*95. SHRI SATAV RAJEEV:\nSHRI MOHITE PATIL VIJAYSINH SHANKARRAO:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has taken cognizance of the recent study by the Centre\nfor Science and Environment which found 85 per cent of bread a nd bakery items\ncontaining potassium bromate;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken or proposed to be taken in this\nregard;\n(c) whether the international agency including World Health Organisation, Food and\nAgriculture Organisation and International Agency for Research on Cancer have also\ndeclared it to be potentially carcinogenic and imposed ban in many countries and if so,\nthe details thereof;\n(d) whether the Government proposes to ban the use of potassium bromate as a food\nadditive and if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) the other corrective steps being taken by the Government in this regard and to\nprevent use of hazardous food additives in the future?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 95* FOR 22ND JULY, 2016\n(a) & (b) The Government is aware of the study by the Centre for Science and\nEnvironment. Potassium bromate was earlier permitted to be used as an additive for\nmanufacturing   bread and other bakery products under the erstwhile Prevention of\nFood Adulteration Act, 1954.\n(c) Potassium bromate was formally withdrawn from the combined Compendi um of\nFood Additives of  Food and Agriculture Organization/ World Health Organization\nin   2011. In several countries, the use of potassium bromate in bread is not permitted.\nIn United States of America (USA), potassium bromate is typically used as a flo ur\nimprover and an oxidizing agent. United States Food and Drugs Authority (USFDA)\ncontinues to allow use of potassium bromate in bread.\n(d) & (e) As part of the exercise for harmonization of food additives with Codex\nGeneral Standards for Food Additives, the Scientific Panel of the Food Safety and\nStandards Authority of India (FSSAI) recommended  removal of potassium bromate\nfrom the list of permitted additives in October, 2015. FSSAI, in its meeting held on\n27th January 2016, approved removal of potassiu m bromate from the list of additives.\nFSSAI has since operationalized the new list of additives that does not include\npotassium bromate as an additive with effect from 20th June 2016.\n*********",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_96": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED  QUESTION NO. *96  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  22.07.2016 \n \nCRECHE POLICY FOR UNORGANISED SECTOR \n \n*96. SHRIMATI  RANJEET  RANJAN: \n \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to State: \n \n(a) Whether the Government proposes to devise any crèche policy for the unorganised sector in urban areas of the \ncountry and if so, the salient features thereof; \n \n(b) the incentives/facilities proposed to be provided to the implementing agencies including women for starting such \ncrèche and day care facilities; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to issue any directions to private and public sector organisations to establish \ncrèche or day care centres in their offices to help new mothers reintegrate into the organisations after  maternity \nleave; and \n \n(d) If so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI MANEKA SANJAY GANDHI) \n \n(a)to(d):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to Part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No.96 for 22.07.2016 by Shrimati \nRanjeet Ranjan, regarding ‘Creche Policy for Unorganised Sector’ \n \n  The Government has set up a Working Group for formulating comprehensive \nprogramme/policy/guidelines for National Programme for Creche and Day Care Facilities for the children \nand its funding pattern etc. under the Chairpersonship of Secretary, WCD with par ticipation from \nMinistry/Department/Organizations and State/UT Governments. \n \nThe above proposal envisages inter -alia the facilities of early childhood care, early stimulation for \nchildren below 3 years , pre-school education (3 -6 years), health care service s including immunization & \ngrowth monitoring, supplementary nutrition, sleeping facilities, drinking water & sanitation facilities. The \nWorking Group would also deliberate upon incentives/ facilities to be provided to implementing agencies. \n \nIt is expected  that the proposed programme would be applicable across the country including \nunorganized sector/private and public sector organizations.   \n \n \n**** \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_97": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 97\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016\nPROMOTION OF SIDDHA SYSTEM OF MEDICINE\n*97. SHRI M. CHANDRAKASI:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any study was carried out to identify advantages and uniqueness of\nSiddha system of medicine as compared to other systems of medici ne and if so, the\ndetails thereof;\n(b) the details of proposals worked out/ pending with the Ministry to promote and\npopularise treatment through Siddha system of medicine; and\n(c) the funds allocated for above purposes during 2016-17?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO 97 FOR 22ND JULY, 2016\n(a) Every system of medicine under AYUSH has its own advantages and\nuniqueness. However, no comparative study to identify the advantages and\nuniqueness of Siddha system as compared to other systems of medicine was carried\nout.\n(b) The Siddha system of medicine is promoted and popularized through\nprogrammes and activities of Central Council for Research in Siddha (CCRS),\nChennai, and the National Institute of Siddha (NIS), Chennai, as well as the National\nAyush Mission (NAM). At present , no proposal in this regard is pending in the\nMinistry.\n(c) CCRS, Chennai, has been allocated Rs.13.00 Crore in Plan and Rs.15.00 Crore in\nNon-Plan and NIS, Chennai , has been allocat ed Rs.28.00 Crore in Plan for its\nactivities which include funds to promote and popularize Siddha System of\nMedicine during the year 2016 -17. Similarly, proposals amounting to Rs. 7.61\nCrores have been approved under NAM to promote treatment through Siddha\nSystem of Medicine during 2016-17.",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_98": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 98\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016\nKIDNEY TRADE\n*98. ADV. M. UDHAYAKUMAR:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has taken note of illegal trade in kidney reported recently\nin Delhi and other hospitals in the country;\n(b) whether the prevalence of illegal organ trade is a result of increasing gap between\nthe demand and supply of organs for transplants across the country and if so, the\ndetails thereof; and\n(c) the steps taken by the Government to bridge this gap including re -visiting and\nrevising guidelines for cadaver -based donations for the benefit of pati ents in urgent\nneed of transplants?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 98* FOR 22ND JULY, 2016\n(a)  Yes.\n(b) The huge gap between the availability of and requirement for organs contributes to the illegal\ntrade in human organs.\n(c)  Health is a State subject. However, the Government of India has, in close cooperation with the\nStates, taken a number of steps to increase donation of cadaver organs to bridge the gap between the\ndemand for and supply of organs and to save the lives of a large number of persons suffering from\nend stage organ failure. The Government of India has, as part of t he National Organ Transplant\nProgrammes, set up an apex national level organization namely National Organs and Tissues\nTransplant Organisation (NOTTO) at New Delhi to promote retrieval of organs and tissues. Updated\ninformation on organ donation related ar eas is made available to the general public on NOTTO\nwebsite. NOTTO has a 24x7 call centre with toll free helpline number (1800114770) and a National\nOrgan and Tissue Donation and Transplant Registry has also been launched. Financial assistance has\nalso be en sanctioned for establishing four Regional Organ and Tissue Transplant Organization\n(ROTTO) in Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, Assam and Chandigarh. Organ donation awareness\nprogrammes and training of transplant coordinators, etc have been taken up in a big way.\n*********"
  }
}